# Golden-section search

`GoldenSection` minimizes a function of one scalar variable by repeatedly
reducing a finite interval. It compares two interior objective values and
reuses one evaluation after each reduction, without derivatives or an
interpolation model.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/golden_section/struct.GoldenSection.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                              |
  | ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
  | Objective   | A deterministic scalar function                          |
  | Evaluations | `CostFunction`                                           |
  | Search      | Local interval reduction, ideally on a unimodal function  |
  | Constraints | Scalar box bounds with finite `lower < upper`             |
  | Parameters  | Scalar `f32` or `f64`, without optional features           |

## When to use it

Consider golden-section search when a minimum lies in a known interval and
you want predictable progress from objective comparisons. It does not need
smoothness or a derivative, making it useful for unimodal functions with
kinks. [Brent](../brent/) usually needs fewer evaluations on smooth objectives
because it adds parabolic interpolation. Compare [Brent with
derivatives](../brent-derivative/) when reliable derivatives are cheap.

The interval should contain a function that decreases toward a minimum and
increases afterward, or is monotone toward a boundary minimum. Multiple local
minima, noise, or broad flat regions can cause comparisons to discard a better
region. A small final interval alone does not certify global optimality.

## How it works

Golden-section search belongs to the sequential interval-search methods of
[Kiefer (1953)](#ref-kiefer-1953). Basin uses the one-dimensional procedure
described in [Press et al. (1992)](#ref-press-etal-1992), §10.1.

For an interval `[a, b]`, let `r = (sqrt(5) - 1) / 2`, approximately `0.618`.
The two interior points are `c1 = a + (1 - r) * (b - a)` and
`c2 = a + r * (b - a)`. If `f(c1) <= f(c2)`, discard the region to the right
of `c2`; otherwise, discard the region to the left of `c1`.

The retained interval has `r` times the previous width, up to rounding. The
golden ratio places one old interior point at the correct position for the
next pair, so only one new objective evaluation is needed per iteration.
The interval width converges linearly.

## Example

The unconstrained minimum of `(x - 2)^2` is at `2`. Restricting the interval to
`[3, 5]` moves the solution to its lower boundary, `3`, with cost `1`.
Golden-section search approaches that boundary from the interior.

<!-- compile: solver-golden-section -->
```rust
use basin::{
    BoxBounds, ConstraintDescription, ConstraintSet, Constraints,
    CostFunction, Executor, GoldenSection, PointState, TerminationCode,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Quadratic {
    lower: f64,
    upper: f64,
}

impl CostFunction for Quadratic {
    type Param = f64;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &f64) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok((x - 2.0).powi(2))
    }
}

impl Constraints for Quadratic {
    type Set = ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<f64>>;

    fn constraints(&self) -> <Self::Set as ConstraintDescription>::Borrowed<'_> {
        ConstraintSet::new().with_bounds(BoxBounds::new(
            Some(&self.lower),
            Some(&self.upper),
        ))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let problem = Quadratic { lower: 3.0, upper: 5.0 };
    let solver = GoldenSection::new()
        .with_absolute_position_tolerance(1e-10);
    let result = Executor::new(problem, solver, PointState::new(4.0))
        .max_iter(100)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert_eq!(result.report.code(), TerminationCode::SolverConverged);
    assert!((3.0..=5.0).contains(result.best_param()));
    assert!((result.best_param() - 3.0).abs() < 1e-6);
    assert!((result.best_cost() - 1.0).abs() < 1e-6);
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Let the interval determine the search

Declare the complete constraint set as `ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<F>>`, with
both finite endpoints present and `lower < upper`. Linear and nonlinear
constraint blocks are unsupported. Use a scale where interval arithmetic
remains finite, and return finite objective values at all sampled points.

Golden-section search ignores the starting point in `PointState`; the bounds
determine its two initial interior points. It does not separately evaluate
the endpoints. A monotone objective therefore gives an approximation to its
boundary minimum rather than an explicit endpoint comparison.

For an unknown interval,
[`MinimumBracketer`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/bracket/minimum/struct.MinimumBracketer.html)
can search for an interior minimum bracket; see the [Brent
example](../brent/#example). Check `bracketed()` before using its outer
endpoints. A boundary minimum need not produce an interior bracket, so supply
known domain bounds directly in that case.

### Choose a width tolerance and a budget

The native test is `b - a <= 2 * tol`, where
`tol = relative * abs(x) + absolute` and `x` is the better of the two current
interior points. `with_absolute_position_tolerance` defaults to `1e-12`, and
`with_relative_position_tolerance` defaults to `sqrt(ε)` for the scalar type.
Both must be finite and strictly positive; invalid settings panic.

Ignoring rounding, the interval width after `n` iterations is its initial
width times `r^n`. Budget enough iterations for that reduction with
`Executor::max_iter`, or limit objective requests with `max_cost_evals`.
Initialization costs two evaluations, and each completed iteration adds one.
Check `result.report` to distinguish a budget stop from native convergence.

Tolerances measure position resolution. They do not bound objective error or
compensate for noisy comparisons. With `f32`, account for its larger machine
epsilon when choosing meaningful accuracy.

### Read the best recorded objective

The current `param()` and `cost()` describe the new probe, which can be worse
than a retained point. Use `best_param()` and `best_cost()` for the lowest
recorded objective, especially if the executor stops before interval
convergence.

Optional cost-change and step-change tolerances compare successive published
probes. They combine with the native test using OR and can stop before the
interval reaches the requested width. Golden-section search publishes no
gradient, so gradient convergence settings are absent from its API.

## Implementation

`PointState<F, F>` publishes each evaluated trial. The solver owns the
interval, two interior points, and their cached costs. A fresh run rebuilds
them from the bounds; an exact solver-and-state checkpoint preserves them
alongside evaluation counts. Automatic bracketing counts its work separately.

Scalar `f32` and `f64` require no linear-algebra backend or optional feature
and work on WASM.

